/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A residential building is constructed 50 m from an elevated urban railway. Ground vibration measurements record a dominant frequency of 25 Hz with displacement amplitude 40 micrometers. Determine: \(a\) peak particle velocity \(PPV\), \(b\) peak acceleration in g's, \(c\) evaluate human annoyance and structural risk against ISO 2631 standards.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Parameters:) Tj
T*
(   - Frequency f = 25 Hz; Angular frequency omega = 2 * pi * f = 2 * pi * 25 = 157.08 rad/s.) Tj
T*
(   - Displacement amplitude: A = 40 micrometers = 40 x 10^-6 m = 0.040 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) Peak Particle Velocity \(PPV\):) Tj
T*
(   - PPV = omega * A = \(157.08 rad/s\) * \(40 x 10^-6 m\) = 6.283 x 10^-3 m/s = 6.28 mm/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) Peak Acceleration a_max:) Tj
T*
(   - a_max = omega^2 * A = \(157.08\)^2 * \(40 x 10^-6\) = \(24,674\) * \(40 x 10^-6\) = 0.987 m/s^2.) Tj
T*
(   - In g's \(g = 9.81 m/s^2\): a_max = 0.987 / 9.81 = 0.1006 g \(~ 0.10 g\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. \(c\) ISO 2631 Human Annoyance and DIN 4150 Structural Assessment:) Tj
T*
(   - Structural Integrity \(DIN 4150-3\): For residential buildings at 25 Hz, the cosmetic cracking) Tj
T*
(   threshold is PPV = 5.0 to 15.0 mm/s.) Tj
T*
(   * The measured 6.28 mm/s is close to the lower limit, indicating possible long-term plaster) Tj
T*
(   hairline cracking.) Tj
T*
(   - Human Perception \(ISO 2631-2\):) Tj
T*
(   * Human perception threshold is PPV ~ 0.15 mm/s. A PPV of 6.28 mm/s is classified as 'severe) Tj
T*
(   disturbance and acute complaints during night-time'.) Tj
T*
(   - Remediation: Install sub-ballast elastomeric matting beneath the rail track or continuous) Tj
T*
(   vertical bentonite trench wave barriers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 24:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the working principles of Ultrasonic Pulse-Echo testing for detecting fatigue cracks in steel bridge pins and eyebars.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Fatigue Failure in Bridge Pins:) Tj
T*
(   - Pin-connected truss bridges rely on forged structural steel pins \(diameter 100 to 400 mm\) to) Tj
T*
(   connect diagonal and chord members.) Tj
T*
(   - Cyclic traffic loading creates shear fretting fatigue cracks within the shear planes hidden) Tj
T*
(   inside the pinhole joints.) Tj
T*
(   - Visual inspection is impossible without disassembling the bridge.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Pulse-Echo Testing Principle:) Tj
T*
(   - An ultrasonic transducer is placed on the accessible end face of the pin.) Tj
T*
(   - High-frequency ultrasonic longitudinal or angled shear waves \(2.25 to 5.0 MHz\) are pulsed) Tj
T*
(   down the cylindrical axis of the pin.) Tj
T*
(   - In a sound pin, the pulse travels to the far end face \(distance L\) and reflects back as a) Tj
T*
(   clean 'back-wall echo' at time t = 2*L / v.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Crack Detection:) Tj
T*
(   - When a fatigue crack exists at distance d < L along a shear plane, acoustic energy reflects) Tj
T*
(   prematurely from the crack interface.) Tj
T*
(   - An echo appears on the oscilloscope screen at time t_crack = 2*d / v prior to the back-wall) Tj
T*
(   echo.) Tj
T*
(   - The amplitude of the flaw echo correlates directly with crack depth.) Tj
T*
(   - Enables detection of hairline fatigue cracks as small as 1 mm without closing the bridge to) Tj
T*
(   traffic.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
