(   [Problem Statement]: An ultrasonic flaw detector inspects a 250 mm thick structural steel plate. The longitudinal wave speed in steel is 5900 m/s. An echo is detected at transit time 42.4 microseconds, while the back-wall echo arrives at 84.7 microseconds. Calculate: \(a\) depth of the internal flaw, \(b\) verified thickness of the steel plate.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Data:) Tj
T*
(   - Longitudinal velocity in steel: v = 5900 m/s = 5.90 mm/microsecond.) Tj
T*
(   - Round-trip transit time to flaw: t_flaw = 42.4 microseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - Round-trip transit time to back-wall: t_back = 84.7 microseconds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) Depth of Internal Flaw d_flaw:) Tj
T*
(   - d_flaw = v * t_flaw / 2 = \( 5.90 mm/us * 42.4 us \) / 2 = 250.16 / 2 = 125.08 mm ~= 125.1 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - The defect lies precisely at mid-thickness \(125 mm deep\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) Plate Thickness T:) Tj
T*
(   - T = v * t_back / 2 = \( 5.90 mm/us * 84.7 us \) / 2 = 499.73 / 2 = 249.87 mm ~= 250.0 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Defect Characterization:) Tj
T*
(   - The echo at 125 mm indicates mid-plane laminar segregation or non-metallic inclusion rolling) Tj
T*
(   flaw.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the working principles of Ultrasonic Cavitation and its industrial applications in concrete aggregate cleaning and soil remediation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mechanism of Ultrasonic Cavitation:) Tj
T*
(   - When intense high-power ultrasound \(> 20 kHz, intensity > 2 W/cm^2\) propagates through a) Tj
T*
(   liquid, it creates alternating high-pressure compression cycles and low-pressure rarefaction) Tj
T*
(   cycles.) Tj
T*
(   - During rarefaction, local pressure drops below the vapor pressure of the liquid, tearing the) Tj
T*
(   liquid apart to form microscopic vapor bubbles \(cavitation nuclei\).) Tj
T*
(   - In subsequent compression cycles, the bubbles collapse violently within picoseconds.) Tj
T*
(   - Transient Collapse Effects: Localized temperatures exceed 5,000 K, pressures exceed 1,000) Tj
T*
(   atmospheres \(100 MPa\), and micro-jets of liquid shoot at velocities > 400 km/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Civil Engineering Applications:) Tj
T*
(   - Aggregate Decontamination: Cavitation micro-jets scrub deleterious clay coatings and organic) Tj
T*
(   silt from recycled concrete aggregates \(RCA\), restoring aggregate-cement bonding.) Tj
T*
(   - Soil Remediation: Breaks down persistent organic pollutants \(PAHs, heavy petroleum) Tj
T*
(   hydrocarbons\) in contaminated brownfield construction sites through localized sonochemical) Tj
T*
(   oxidation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 29:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A 400 mm thick concrete beam is tested with UPV. A pulse velocity of 4000 m/s is recorded in dry concrete. When saturated with water, the density increases by 3% and dynamic modulus increases by 8%. Calculate the new pulse velocity and explain why moist concrete has higher UPV.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Governing Formula for Pulse Velocity:) Tj
T*
(   - v_p = sqrt\( E_d * F\(nu\) / rho \), where F\(nu\) = \(1 - nu\) / [ \(1 + nu\)*\(1 - 2*nu\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Assume Poisson factor F\(nu\) is approximately constant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Relative Change Calculation:) Tj
T*
(   - Density increases by 3%: rho_new = 1.03 * rho_old.) Tj
T*
ET

